the strike against General Motors is  already taking a toll on the country's  largest automaker GM stocks slid more  than four percent yesterday  as more than 48,000 workers walked off  the job some estimates suggest GM is  losing as much as 50 million dollars a  day Dean Reynolds is at a GM assembly  plant in Detroit  Dean is any progress being made good  morning well a source told us that  yesterday's meeting was very tense so if  there is any progress it's happening  very slowly and the workers here told us  that they will choose the picket line  over the assembly line for as long as it  takes so far workers here see little  reason for optimism as the strike enters  its second day the corporation's make a  lot of money right now we just ax apply  General Motors made 8.1 billion dollars  in profit last year Patricia brown has  worked at GM for 34 years you want to do  all the work what do you think about  that they just sit back behind that desk  Union Auto Workers like her are looking  at only 250 dollars a week in strike pay  about 625 an hour GM has proposed over 7  billion dollars in investments more than  5,400 new jobs and wage increases but  union leadership told us only 2% of  workers demands have been resolved since  negotiations began in July a major point  of contention is health coverage  according to an automotive news report  Union Auto Workers pay just 3 to 4  percent of their medical costs and GM's  opening proposal called on them to  increase that to 15 percent automakers  see rising costs and they want some more  contribution from workers Cox automotive  executive analyst Michelle Krebs says  the divide between GM and the Union  comes as the industry faces major  changes how many jobs will exist because  of electric vehicles autonomous vehicles  Automation those are issues of real  concern now GM says its goal quote  remains to reach an agreement that  builds a stronger future for the company  and its employees and that includes what  they call nationally leading benefits in  the health care coverage but GM is  offering few details on that point  Gail alright Dean thank you very much
 

